Clinic /29 Exhaustion & PAT (NAT Overload) Table Example:
  203.0.113.8/29 provides only 6 usable public IPs (203.0.113.9 - 203.0.113.14) for 18 devices.
  With PAT/NAPT, two internal PCs both using source port 50000 get unique external ports:
  +------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+
  | Inside Local           | Inside Global (NAT)    | Outside Global         |
  +------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+
  | 192.168.1.10:50000     | 203.0.113.10:50000     | 93.184.216.34:443      |
  | 192.168.1.11:50000     | 203.0.113.10:50001     | 93.184.216.34:443      |
  +------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+

Datagram vs Virtual-Circuit Switching:
  - Datagram (IP)     : Each packet routed independently; multipath/rerouting causes out-of-order arrival.
  - Virtual Circuit   : Fixed pre-established path/label (MPLS/ATM); packets follow identical path in order.

--- Alternative Concept Analysis: Public /27 vs /29 + NAT ---
  Public /27 usable IPs : 30 hosts (fits 18 devices directly with 12 spares)
  Cost & Exposure       : Higher monthly IP lease cost; all 18 devices directly reachable/scannable on internet (needs strict stateful firewall).
  NAT Problems Removed  : Fixes inbound P2P/VoIP/SIP ALG breakage, port-forwarding conflicts, and end-to-end IPsec/checksum issues.
